In `Lexer.next_token()`:
When `c` is `system.execute`, `std.ascii.isAlphabetic(c)` matches `s`, and `self.cursor` consumes `system.execute`.
`std.mem.eql(u8, text, "system.execute")` returns `.KwSystemExecute`!
Then in `compile_source`:
`else if (tok.kind == .KwSystemExecute ...)`
`tok = lex.next_token();` -> `(`
`tok = lex.next_token();` -> `raw_untrusted`
`arg_var` = `raw_untrusted`!
In `symbols`:
`raw_untrusted` was stored when `val raw_untrusted: candidate<ToolCall> = ...` was parsed!
When `val raw_untrusted: candidate<ToolCall>` was parsed:
`is_tainted` was set to `true`!

Let's verify why `is_tainted` check on `raw_untrusted` didn't fail:
When `val raw_untrusted: candidate<ToolCall> = model.infer(...)` was parsed:
`tok` was `.KwVal` -> `tok` became `.Identifier` (`raw_untrusted`) -> `tok` became `.Colon` -> `tok` became `.KwCandidate` -> `type_kind = .CandidateTainted`, `is_tainted = true` -> `skip <ToolCall>` -> `tok` became `.Equals` -> `tok` became `.Identifier` (`model.infer`)!
Because `model.infer` was an `.Identifier`!
In `compile_source`:
```zig
                if (tok.kind == .KwGate) {
                    is_gated = true;
                    is_tainted = false;
                } else if (tok.kind == .Identifier) {
                    const rhs_var = tok.text;
                    for (0..self.symbol_count) |s| {
